   "etymology": "Uncertain. Most credibly akin to ënjtem (“to fill up, swell from eating”), dialectally enj, ënj ~ âj (“to cause to swell”), in reference to Fat Thursday, as proposed by Topalli (2017).\nOutdated theories\nTreimer (1923) assumes the existence of a pagan god *Enji, which must have been the most important, given how it was used to calque Juppiter, akin to Sanskrit अग्नि (agni), Hindu god of fire. This is endorsed by Orel (1998).\nOštir (1925) tentatively connects to Etruscan 𐌀𐌉𐌔 (ais, “god”) or to 𐌖𐌍𐌉 (uni, “Juno”).\nMjeda (1932) derives it from Ancient Greek ἕνη (hénē), comparing its use in the expression Ancient Greek ἕνη καὶ νέα (hénē kaì néa, “the last day of the month”).",
